WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.125 --> 00:00:03.128
Can you believe that as
a 28-year-old unmarried woman,

00:00:03.211 --> 00:00:06.047
I'm literally too old
to be called a spinster?

00:00:06.131 --> 00:00:07.507
The correct term for someone

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:10.927
in my apparently geriatric
state is a thornback.

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:12.929
Society is so
obsessed with marriage

00:00:13.012 --> 00:00:14.681
that not only do
we define ourselves

00:00:14.764 --> 00:00:15.974
by our relationship status,

00:00:16.057 --> 00:00:17.475
which was like 42%
of my motivation

00:00:17.559 --> 00:00:20.353
for getting my doctorate
in the first place,

00:00:20.437 --> 00:00:22.272
but we've created a social ideal

00:00:22.355 --> 00:00:24.482
wherein the only way to
truly live happily ever after

00:00:24.566 --> 00:00:28.194
is to fall in love
and stay that way.

00:00:28.278 --> 00:00:31.030
Yet nearly half of marriages
in the U.S. end in divorce

00:00:31.114 --> 00:00:33.616
because love doesn't
always last forever.

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:36.578
I should know, I was
engaged and head over heels

00:00:36.661 --> 00:00:38.371
before a bad breakup last year.

00:00:38.455 --> 00:00:39.998
Everyone in my life expected me

00:00:40.081 --> 00:00:43.251
to be a devastated wreck for
weeks, and I was at first,

00:00:43.334 --> 00:00:45.879
but it didn't take long for me
to see the silver linings.

00:00:45.962 --> 00:00:47.839
At the time, I was
comforted by a story

00:00:47.922 --> 00:00:48.923
about a fellow thornback,

00:00:49.007 --> 00:00:52.177
the Hawaiian volcano
goddess Pelehonuamea,

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:53.928
Pele to her friends,

00:00:54.012 --> 00:00:57.098
who found love but gained so
much more when it was lost.

00:00:57.182 --> 00:00:58.433
It's an interesting story,

00:00:58.516 --> 00:01:00.643
even if you haven't recently
had your heart broken,

00:01:00.727 --> 00:01:05.690
full of magical fight scenes,
canoes and even murder.

00:01:05.774 --> 00:01:08.401
[spooky music]

00:01:10.195 --> 00:01:11.613
Maybe you've heard of Pele.

00:01:11.696 --> 00:01:14.949
She's a major figure in Hawaiian
mythology and pop culture,

00:01:15.033 --> 00:01:18.203
allegedly the inspiration for
Te Fiti in Disney's "Moana"

00:01:18.286 --> 00:01:20.246
because Te Fiti is
not a real goddess

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:21.873
in Polynesian folklore.

00:01:21.956 --> 00:01:24.959
But most people don't know
that Pele was briefly entangled

00:01:25.043 --> 00:01:28.046
with a shape-shifting
demigod named Kamapua'a.

00:01:28.129 --> 00:01:30.048
Some would call
him Pele's husband,

00:01:30.131 --> 00:01:31.966
but that's not really
an appropriate label

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:35.512
because Hawaiians didn't have
a western concept of marriage.

00:01:35.595 --> 00:01:36.846
There were unions

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:38.556
that could be blessed
by gods and the chief,

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:40.266
but they weren't legally binding

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:42.268
and divorce just wasn't a thing.

00:01:42.352 --> 00:01:44.229
They don't really teach
us this in history class,

00:01:44.312 --> 00:01:46.272
but Hawaii
was a sovereign nation

00:01:46.356 --> 00:01:47.982
with its own
language and culture

00:01:48.066 --> 00:01:50.527
before colonization
in the 1700s.

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:53.279
They were an oral society
passing their knowledge

00:01:53.363 --> 00:01:55.406
about how the world works
onto the next generation

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:57.408
through stories, or mo'olelo.

00:01:57.492 --> 00:02:00.370
For example, the
mo'olelo used Kamapua'a

00:02:00.453 --> 00:02:02.664
and Pele's relationship
as an explanation

00:02:02.747 --> 00:02:05.625
for Hawaii's rich
ecological tapestry.

00:02:05.708 --> 00:02:09.462
In myth, Pele is depicted as
the divine embodiment of lava

00:02:09.546 --> 00:02:12.966
who is equally likely
to aid or unalive you.

00:02:13.049 --> 00:02:15.385
A true chaotic neutral
on the alignment chart,

00:02:15.468 --> 00:02:18.388
Hawaiians revered Pele as
the creator of their islands

00:02:18.471 --> 00:02:21.474
just as much as they feared
the destruction she caused.

00:02:21.558 --> 00:02:23.810
There are many versions
of Pele's story,

00:02:23.893 --> 00:02:26.396
but in most of them, Pele
was born to divine parents

00:02:26.479 --> 00:02:29.524
in the legendary ancestral
homeland, Kahiki.

00:02:29.607 --> 00:02:33.152
Through some linguistic
magic, those Ks become Ts,

00:02:33.236 --> 00:02:34.153
and you see the real history

00:02:34.237 --> 00:02:36.698
of Polynesian
migration from Tahiti

00:02:36.781 --> 00:02:39.242
reflected in Hawaiian mo'olelo.

00:02:39.325 --> 00:02:40.660
Pele had many siblings

00:02:40.743 --> 00:02:43.413
with their own abilities to
control the sea or clouds,

00:02:43.496 --> 00:02:46.249
but she took after
her uncle Lonomakua,

00:02:46.332 --> 00:02:48.334
who taught her how
to control fire.

00:02:48.418 --> 00:02:52.171
Even as a child, her immense
power, or mana, was obvious.

00:02:52.255 --> 00:02:53.423
And not that it matters,

00:02:53.506 --> 00:02:55.508
but she was also said
to be quite beautiful.

00:02:55.592 --> 00:02:59.220
One of Pele's sisters, a sea
goddess named Namakaokaha'i,

00:02:59.304 --> 00:03:02.265
grew jealous and chased
Pele from Kahiki.

00:03:02.348 --> 00:03:05.435
In some versions, Namaka
resents Pele's strength,

00:03:05.518 --> 00:03:08.813
and in others, Pele has an
affair with Namaka's partner.

00:03:10.148 --> 00:03:11.190
Why are you chasing me?

00:03:11.274 --> 00:03:12.692
Because you stole my husband.

00:03:12.775 --> 00:03:13.776
It wasn't me.

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:15.069
Because you set
my house on fire.

00:03:15.153 --> 00:03:15.987
It wasn't me.

00:03:16.070 --> 00:03:17.447
Because you're just too strong.

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:20.450
I swear on my life
that it wasn't me!

00:03:22.911 --> 00:03:23.953
Gasp!

00:03:25.955 --> 00:03:27.540
Okay, maybe it was me.

00:03:27.624 --> 00:03:30.209
Pele fled from her sister
with a few of her siblings

00:03:30.293 --> 00:03:33.129
trying to find a new home
for them and her fire.

00:03:33.212 --> 00:03:36.424
Several times she dug into
the earth to build a fire pit,

00:03:36.507 --> 00:03:39.385
only to be flooded by her
sister's salty revenge.

00:03:39.469 --> 00:03:43.348
Pele moved southeast from
Kauai to Oahu and then to Maui,

00:03:43.431 --> 00:03:45.934
where she ultimately succumbed
to her sister's attack.

00:03:46.017 --> 00:03:49.354
But mana as strong as Pele's
can't be stopped by death.

00:03:49.437 --> 00:03:51.689
Free from her more human form,

00:03:51.773 --> 00:03:54.651
Pele's fiery spirit traveled
to the Big Island of Hawaii

00:03:54.734 --> 00:03:56.361
to make her last stand.

00:03:56.444 --> 00:03:58.655
Pele's lava made land that
traced the path of her voyage,

00:03:58.738 --> 00:04:01.449
and now she lives
with her family

00:04:01.532 --> 00:04:03.910
in Hawaii's active
Kilauea Volcano.

00:04:03.993 --> 00:04:06.704
Kamapua'a's origins
are just as rocky,

00:04:06.788 --> 00:04:09.666
figuratively speaking, since
geology is in his domain.

00:04:09.749 --> 00:04:12.335
He is another major figure
in Hawaiian folklore

00:04:12.418 --> 00:04:14.754
known as the hog god of Oahu

00:04:14.837 --> 00:04:17.924
and associated with
fertility and agriculture.

00:04:18.007 --> 00:04:20.677
Again, there are many
versions of the story,

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:23.888
but by most accounts,
Kamapua'a was born on Oahu

00:04:23.972 --> 00:04:26.808
to his mother Hina,
the goddess of the moon.

00:04:26.891 --> 00:04:28.643
Hina was married
to Chief Olopana,

00:04:28.726 --> 00:04:30.395
but was crushing hard

00:04:30.478 --> 00:04:32.981
on his more attractive
younger brother.

00:04:33.064 --> 00:04:34.732
Born into this
Jon Snow situation

00:04:34.816 --> 00:04:36.234
through no fault of his own,

00:04:36.317 --> 00:04:39.696
these daddy issues led
Kamapua'a down a violent path.

00:04:39.779 --> 00:04:40.822
Like the better
known demigod Maui,

00:04:40.905 --> 00:04:45.326
Kamapua'a was a kupua,
a magical shape shifter.

00:04:45.410 --> 00:04:46.786
In his human form,

00:04:46.869 --> 00:04:50.456
Kamapua'a was handsome and
charming, albeit quick to anger,

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:51.624
but he could shift
at will into a hog

00:04:51.708 --> 00:04:55.003
as well as certain
fish and plants.

00:04:55.086 --> 00:04:56.963
Heartbroken over his
father's disgust,

00:04:57.046 --> 00:05:00.008
Kamapua'a started
raiding Olopana's lands.

00:05:00.091 --> 00:05:01.426
In some stories,

00:05:01.509 --> 00:05:04.095
he makes his human form more
closely resemble his hog shape

00:05:04.178 --> 00:05:06.180
by cutting his hair
short and spiky,

00:05:06.264 --> 00:05:07.890
tattooing his body
with dark ink,

00:05:07.974 --> 00:05:10.727
and wearing a jacket with
spines down the back.

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:14.105
Kamapua'a was apprehended
and released multiple times

00:05:14.188 --> 00:05:17.275
until he was blinded enough
by his fury to kill the chief,

00:05:17.358 --> 00:05:21.571
and much like Pele, flee to
the next island in the chain.

00:05:21.654 --> 00:05:23.823
He hopped from one
island to another,

00:05:23.906 --> 00:05:25.283
picking fights on each one

00:05:25.366 --> 00:05:28.327
and earning himself a nasty
reputation as a brute.

00:05:28.411 --> 00:05:30.872
Finally, he came to
Hawaii where he saw Pele

00:05:30.955 --> 00:05:33.624
and was instantly
enamored of her beauty.

00:05:33.708 --> 00:05:37.462
Both Pele and Kamapua'a
had strong personalities.

00:05:37.545 --> 00:05:39.964
He was unapologetically
crass and violent,

00:05:40.048 --> 00:05:42.425
and she had little
patience to suffer fools,

00:05:42.508 --> 00:05:45.678
especially one who was supposed
to be her equal partner.

00:05:45.762 --> 00:05:49.057
They were simultaneously too
similar and too different,

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:51.309
clashing in ways that
make great entertainment,

00:05:51.392 --> 00:05:52.894
but a terrible relationship.

00:05:52.977 --> 00:05:55.063
Kamapua'a stood atop
the volcano crater

00:05:55.146 --> 00:05:56.439
where he first saw Pele

00:05:56.522 --> 00:05:59.067
and tried to woo her
with songs and dancing,

00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:01.944
but Pele, foreshadowing
the rise of Shania Twain,

00:06:02.028 --> 00:06:03.488
was not much impressed.

00:06:03.571 --> 00:06:06.324
"You're a pig and the son
of a pig!" she derided him,

00:06:06.407 --> 00:06:09.285
which sent Kamapua'a
into a fit of rage.

00:06:09.368 --> 00:06:13.289
Pele is lovely!
Pele is so sweet!

00:06:13.372 --> 00:06:16.626
Pele, you would love me
if only we could meet.

00:06:16.709 --> 00:06:17.877
Ew, that's gross.

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:20.922
You're a pig,
and you're the son of a pig!

00:06:21.005 --> 00:06:23.132
Yeah? Well, your mother
was a hamster

00:06:23.216 --> 00:06:24.842
and your father smells
like elderberries!

00:06:24.926 --> 00:06:26.969
Isn't that from "Monty
Python and the Holy Grail"?

00:06:27.053 --> 00:06:29.055
We don't even have
elderberries in Hawaii,

00:06:29.138 --> 00:06:30.848
and we're about
a thousand years too early

00:06:30.932 --> 00:06:32.100
for that reference!

00:06:32.183 --> 00:06:34.352
I love that, you know that.

00:06:34.435 --> 00:06:36.354
The two hurled
insults at each other

00:06:36.437 --> 00:06:39.982
until Pele volleyed with smoke
and flame instead of words.

00:06:40.066 --> 00:06:41.651
Kamapua'a easily
countered her attack,

00:06:41.734 --> 00:06:44.445
perhaps with weather
powers of his own

00:06:44.529 --> 00:06:46.948
or help from his water
god family members.

00:06:47.031 --> 00:06:48.241
Pele shook the earth

00:06:48.324 --> 00:06:50.868
and sent streams of
lava to burn Kamapua'a,

00:06:50.952 --> 00:06:53.079
who turned into grass
to divert her flows

00:06:53.162 --> 00:06:54.789
and answered with
floods of his own,

00:06:54.872 --> 00:06:57.250
nearly drowning
Pele and her family.

00:06:57.333 --> 00:06:59.168
Battle raged until
the two realized

00:06:59.252 --> 00:07:00.711
neither one had the upper hand

00:07:00.795 --> 00:07:04.674
and their passion transformed
into something more intimate.

00:07:04.757 --> 00:07:05.967
For the first time in his life,

00:07:06.050 --> 00:07:08.261
Kamapua'a felt loved and happy,

00:07:08.344 --> 00:07:09.887
but it wasn't Pele's
responsibility

00:07:09.971 --> 00:07:11.055
to keep him that way.

00:07:11.139 --> 00:07:13.558
Pele realized that their
relationship was toxic,

00:07:13.641 --> 00:07:16.936
though not in the comforting
way that her volcano fumes were

00:07:17.019 --> 00:07:18.062
and that it was keeping her

00:07:18.146 --> 00:07:19.647
from realizing
her true potential

00:07:19.730 --> 00:07:22.275
as Hawaii's eminent
volcano goddess.

00:07:22.358 --> 00:07:23.651
She broke up with Kamapua'a

00:07:23.734 --> 00:07:26.154
and the two agreed to split
the Big Island in half

00:07:26.237 --> 00:07:28.072
so they wouldn't have
to interact again.

00:07:28.156 --> 00:07:31.659
I adore this trope
in fiction and myth.

00:07:31.742 --> 00:07:33.161
Pele stuck to the western side

00:07:33.244 --> 00:07:34.871
where her volcanoes
could generate new earth

00:07:34.954 --> 00:07:36.873
and fertilize the existing land.

00:07:36.956 --> 00:07:38.833
Kamapua'a took the eastern side,

00:07:38.916 --> 00:07:41.419
where his warm rains
could nourish crops.

00:07:41.502 --> 00:07:44.172
This juxtaposition in
climate makes the Big Island

00:07:44.255 --> 00:07:47.884
one of the most ecologically
diverse places on the planet.

00:07:47.967 --> 00:07:50.761
I think the best way to describe
the love these two shared

00:07:50.845 --> 00:07:54.098
is fierce, but even
the fiercest affection

00:07:54.182 --> 00:07:56.392
can crumble in the face
of incompatibility.

00:07:56.475 --> 00:07:58.227
For Pele and Kamapua'a,

00:07:58.311 --> 00:08:00.605
their relationship's
achilles heel was anger.

00:08:00.688 --> 00:08:03.900
For me and my ex, it was
contrasting views on family

00:08:03.983 --> 00:08:06.068
and for millions of
people who break up

00:08:06.152 --> 00:08:07.445
or get divorced each year,

00:08:07.528 --> 00:08:10.281
the final straw could be
adultery, financial stress,

00:08:10.364 --> 00:08:12.158
or a thousand other issues.

00:08:12.241 --> 00:08:14.535
Traditionalists
love to talk about

00:08:14.619 --> 00:08:17.205
the moral decline of our
society by pointing to marriage,

00:08:17.288 --> 00:08:18.915
divorce, and birth rates,

00:08:18.998 --> 00:08:21.375
but I think they're missing
the bigger picture here.

00:08:21.459 --> 00:08:23.127
Yes, marriage rates
are dropping,

00:08:23.211 --> 00:08:25.546
but so are divorce rates.

00:08:25.630 --> 00:08:27.882
Younger generations are
waiting longer to get married,

00:08:27.965 --> 00:08:30.384
making them more mature
and less likely to fall

00:08:30.468 --> 00:08:32.845
into the relationship
pitfalls their parents did.

00:08:32.929 --> 00:08:34.347
And the older generations

00:08:34.430 --> 00:08:36.682
who are experiencing slightly
higher divorce rates,

00:08:36.766 --> 00:08:39.268
mostly initiated by
women, by the way,

00:08:39.352 --> 00:08:41.187
are ending incompatible
relationships

00:08:41.270 --> 00:08:43.356
that likely shouldn't
have been legally bound

00:08:43.439 --> 00:08:44.565
in the first place.

00:08:44.649 --> 00:08:46.567
And if our society
didn't pressure people

00:08:46.651 --> 00:08:49.362
to get married so
young for so long,

00:08:49.445 --> 00:08:50.446
maybe they wouldn't have.

00:08:50.529 --> 00:08:52.240
Is it a coincidence that Hawaii,

00:08:52.323 --> 00:08:53.199
a culture that historically

00:08:53.282 --> 00:08:55.409
didn't have the
concept of marriage,

00:08:55.493 --> 00:08:57.578
has the lowest divorce
rate in the U.S.?

00:08:57.662 --> 00:08:58.955
I don't think so.

00:08:59.038 --> 00:09:01.499
These divorcees are
learning a powerful lesson.

00:09:01.582 --> 00:09:03.501
Breakups are good actually.

00:09:03.584 --> 00:09:06.003
If a relationship isn't
working, it's better to end it

00:09:06.087 --> 00:09:07.922
and be grateful
for the benefits.

00:09:08.005 --> 00:09:11.175
After Pele left him, Kamapua'a
fled to a distant island

00:09:11.259 --> 00:09:12.969
and married a chieftess,

00:09:13.052 --> 00:09:15.846
supposedly getting his
own happily ever after.

00:09:15.930 --> 00:09:18.975
Pele got to realize her dream
of basking in her own glory

00:09:19.058 --> 00:09:21.018
and living with her
fire and family.

00:09:21.102 --> 00:09:22.478
And when my engagement ended,

00:09:22.562 --> 00:09:24.105
I was able to focus
on my friendships

00:09:24.188 --> 00:09:25.773
and eat Nutella again.

00:09:25.856 --> 00:09:28.150
I'm glad mythology is
full of love stories

00:09:28.234 --> 00:09:29.777
and tragedies alike,

00:09:29.860 --> 00:09:32.154
but I wish there were more
stories like this one,

00:09:32.238 --> 00:09:34.573
where losing love
wasn't seen as tragic.

00:09:34.657 --> 00:09:36.367
So at the next
all-millennial meeting

00:09:36.450 --> 00:09:38.536
when we decide which
industry we're going to ruin,

00:09:38.619 --> 00:09:40.955
I'm doubling down on marriage.

00:09:41.038 --> 00:09:43.332
Not because I don't
believe in love; I do,

00:09:43.416 --> 00:09:46.294
but because all of Polynesia
would've been worse off

00:09:46.377 --> 00:09:50.172
if Pele and Kamapua'a had
actually been married.

00:09:50.256 --> 00:09:52.091
You can choose to ignore
this mythical precedent

00:09:52.174 --> 00:09:54.635
if you wish, but I
have learned my lesson.

00:09:54.719 --> 00:09:57.471
I still want to fall in love
and have a big party though.

00:09:57.555 --> 00:10:00.224
It's time for another
fantasy pantheon pick,

00:10:00.308 --> 00:10:02.226
and it is my turn to
choose a new pick.

00:10:02.310 --> 00:10:03.185
Are you ready, Dr. Z?

00:10:03.269 --> 00:10:04.478
-Yes, I'm excited.
-Okay.

00:10:04.562 --> 00:10:08.274
My pick from the Hawaiian
pantheon is Laka.

00:10:08.357 --> 00:10:09.525
-Oh.
-Have you heard of her?

00:10:09.608 --> 00:10:12.445
Is she the one that's
most associated with hula?

00:10:12.528 --> 00:10:13.446
-Yes!
-Yay!

00:10:13.529 --> 00:10:14.363
-Yeah. Yeah.
-Okay.

00:10:14.447 --> 00:10:15.740
She's the goddess of the hula.

00:10:15.823 --> 00:10:17.616
And so she is
a goddess of dance,

00:10:17.700 --> 00:10:20.870
which I think we will
absolutely want in our utopia.

00:10:20.953 --> 00:10:22.288
Of course.

00:10:22.371 --> 00:10:25.249
But she's also a goddess
of forest growth and love,

00:10:25.333 --> 00:10:27.084
and I think that
all of that energy

00:10:27.168 --> 00:10:28.753
is something we want
in our perfect world.

00:10:28.836 --> 00:10:30.129
Those are three
things I really love,

00:10:30.212 --> 00:10:31.630
so I appreciate that pick.

00:10:32.631 --> 00:10:36.635
Accessibility provided by the
U.S. Department of Education.

